.TH GIT-SHOOK 1 "2026-09-05" "git-shook 0.1.0" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git-shook \- run several tools at one git hook event
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git shook install
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B git shook list
.br
.B git shook doctor
.br
.B git shook uninstall
.br
.B git shook version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Git runs one program per hook event, so a tool that installs a hook replaces
whatever was there, with nothing printed and nothing to notice.
.B git-shook
puts one dispatcher at the event and gives every tool a place to register beside
the others.
.PP
A project declares what it wants run in a
.IR shook.toml ,
committed, anywhere in the tree.
.B git shook install
reads every tracked manifest, shows the argument list each one would run, and
registers them once the operator agrees.
.PP
Nothing is installed until
.B install
is run, and manifests are read only then, never when a hook fires. A manifest
arriving through a merge therefore does nothing until somebody looks at it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install " [\-\-yes]"
Read every tracked manifest, print what each would run, and register it. With
.B \-\-yes
it does not ask, which is what a script wants and what a person should not use
the first time.
.TP
.B list
What is registered, per event, in the order it runs.
.TP
.B doctor
Whether what the manifests declare and what is registered still agree. Exits
non\-zero when they do not.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove the registry, clear
.IR core.hooksPath ,
and put back whatever hook was at the event before.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH THE MANIFEST
.PP
.EX
name = "ante"

[hooks.pre\-commit]
run = ["ante", "fix"]
.EE
.PP
.I run
is an argument list rather than a command line. Nothing goes through a shell, so
there is no quoting to get wrong and no way for an argument to become a second
command. A hook needing a pipe or a redirect ships a script and names it, which
puts the thing that actually runs in a file somebody can read in a diff.
.PP
Each hook runs with its own manifest's directory as the working directory.
.I name
decides the filename in the registry and therefore the order, which is sorted
under the C collation; left out, the directory's name is used. A name carrying a
path separator is refused, as is an event git does not have, and two manifests
claiming one name at one event.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I shook.toml
A manifest. Any number of them, anywhere in the tree, and each must be tracked
by git to be discovered.
.TP
.I .git/shook/hooks/
Where
.I core.hooksPath
points. One dispatcher per event. Inside the git directory, so it is neither
committed nor something anybody has to ignore.
.TP
.I .git/shook/entries/<event>/<name>
One registered entry. The first line is the working directory relative to the
repository root, and every line after it is one argument.
.TP
.I .git/shook/kept/<event>
Whatever hook was at the event before, still running as the first entry.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR doctor ,
everything declared is registered.
.TP
.B 1
A manifest was refused, an operation failed, or
.B doctor
found a disagreement.
.SH SECURITY
Git will not read a hooks path out of the repository, deliberately: if it did,
cloning a repository would execute whatever that repository said to execute. The
activation therefore lives in
.I .git/config
and cannot be committed, and running
.B git shook install
is the consent a committed setting could never express.
.PP
For the same reason manifests are read during
.B install
and never when a hook fires.
.SH LIMITATIONS
An argument containing a newline cannot be represented, since an entry file is
one argument per line, and such a manifest is refused rather than silently
turned into two arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR githooks (5),
.BR git-config (1)
.SH AUTHOR
orgrinrt <orgrinrt@ikiuni.dev>
